WEBVTT

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.770
...to set records, prove himself, place himself at the topmost point of his dream, Victory Circle.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:43.350
the sands of Daytona, the world's most famous beach.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:48.890
50 weeks a year, this Florida resort town basks in sunshine

00:00:48.890 --> 00:01:00.000
and listens to the quiet sounds of the surf, high tide,

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:07.390
the Atlantic scours the wide smooth beach with the pressure of a million tons of water and

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:15.270
suspended sand. The sand is dropped before the surf retreats, and at low tide, the sand

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:30.000
dries to become a great natural superhighway, 300 feet wide, 20 miles long. One mile of this beach, one measured mile, becomes

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.020
the most important spot in America

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.450
for automakers, drivers, and mechanics.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.410
For 14 days, every major American stock car

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.350
and many European manufacturers

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:46.640
race the clock in flying mile and acceleration tests.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.560
When these tests are complete,

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.430
the course is changed to include the asphalt road

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:54.110
running parallel to the beach,

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:59.540
and the 160-mile Grand National is then ready to run.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:07.430
First, attention focuses on the flying mile. At the starting line, a Pontiac, brought to

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:12.630
Daytona by a young man who has set the pace of the racing world by owning the cars which

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:19.840
won the last two Indianapolis 500-mile classics. John Zink, who usually hires professional

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:30.000
drivers, is testing this one out himself in a one-way, unofficial run. Zink begins his

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:48.870
one-way run and all the marks 141.2 miles an hour the fastest time ever

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:53.500
recorded on this beach by an American passenger car that's the mark all the

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:58.050
others must shoot for but roaring down the beach in a straightaway run at better

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.000
than a hundred and thirty miles an hour

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.310
Anything can happen.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:06.760
Look out!

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:18.890
Bill Norcutt is trapped in the wreck.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:20.960
His seatbelt held.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.940
And rescue workers must cut him loose.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:40.410
his feet, walking away, race day, and the Air Force salutes the fastest field ever to compete

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:54.530
at Daytona Beach. The largest crowd in Daytona history watches the Air Force show before

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:57.080
turning to the bigger show shaping up on the sand.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:11.250
pace lap of the Grand National Beach and Road Race. 58 high-performance stock

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:17.130
cars parade down the two-mile-long beach straightaway, then wheel into the North

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:24.760
Turn. Pontiac, Oldsmobile, and Mercury with multiple carburetors, superchargers

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:30.000
on boards, high-compression Plymouths, Chevrolets with fuel injection,

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.960
all battling for Victory Circle. They're moving down the backstretch now, a two-lane asphalt road

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:45.070
that will know speeds of 130 miles an hour before the day is over. This 160-mile event

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:52.590
will give a car more punishment than three years driving on the highway. This is the Grand National,

00:04:53.470 --> 00:05:00.000
the grand challenge for endurance, for power, for performance. Here they come into the

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:16.190
south turn and the green flag the race is on through the south turn banjo matthews in number

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:20.640
eight first car off the turn booming down the beach straight away the pace car gets out of

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.760
the way as matthew sends his car screaming north at 125 miles an hour

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:34.920
But there's a challenge.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:38.290
Cotton Owens in number six moves into second place.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:50.220
And he's passing to take the lead.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.180
Number six with Owens at the wheel leading

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:54.550
as number 47 comes up to second place.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:20.310
south turn Bob dual runs number 95 up the bank and out of the race the leaders

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:24.310
hit the beach together and Owens chief mechanic Ray Nichols keeps his fingers

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:30.000
crossed Owens in number six putting a lot of beats between himself and the second place car

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:43.850
look at him go. Coming through the pack from 20th starting position, Paul Goldsmith giving

00:06:43.850 --> 00:07:00.000
his number three a furious ride. Owens leads the way. They come back on the beach. Number 85 is

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:12.110
second and closing in oh there's a spin and another number 85 slams into the spinning car

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:18.280
that's all the racing he'll do today there's another car in trouble and paul goldsmith coming

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:27.540
through just look at that man drive he's in second place tiny whirly co-mechanic on the

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:29.920
lead car is worried pocket radios

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.500
Tune to the race broadcast, tell the crew Goldsmith has closed in on Owens on the backstretch.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:41.790
There they are, number three and number six screaming north on the beach at 126 miles an hour.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.680
Look out, fast cars coming through.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:56.530
Goldsmith takes the lead.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:07.470
trouble. This is the fastest race ever run on this beach and road course, and some

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:14.370
can't take it. Hey, where did everybody go? The course gets rougher as the

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:20.570
afternoon wears on. Goldsmith gives number three a jolting ride to stay in

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:25.990
front. Owens, a former winner here, knows the beach like the back of his hand. He

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:30.000
whips his car through the north turn, less than a half a minute behind number

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:40.890
Ray Nichols, Owen's chief mechanic, spent weeks tearing down, testing, and reassembling Owen's engine.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:44.080
Will it stand the strain of this all-out drive?

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:51.920
Here comes Goldsmith, and there's Owen's closing in.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:03.310
It looks like Goldsmith is having some kind of trouble.

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:07.330
Goldsmith suddenly loses speed,

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:12.400
and Owens sets his sights on a trail of blue-gray smoke down the backstretch.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:29.890
Number three, stopwatches show he must be slowing down.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:41.410
goldsmith limps into the pits giving up first place owens in number six grabs the lead goldsmith's

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:48.070
crew raise the hood there's a burn piston they tried to squeeze just a little too much power

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:54.740
out of that engine it was a great race while it was running but that's all for today

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:58.930
ray nickel signals that the pressure is off telling owens to take it easy

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:30.000
Owens is running for a record, his average speed is better than 101 miles an hour, a new grand national record, and he's roaring toward the checkered flag, he's on his last lap, Owens in number 6 takes the checkered flag.

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:37.270
A triumphal turn over the four-mile beach and road course, then to Victory Circle with

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:43.090
a new record of 101.6 miles an hour.

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:47.980
Mechanic Nichols, long a user of perfect circle piston rings at the Indianapolis Speedway,

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:54.420
depended on them here in setting up the engine that brought him to Daytona's Victory Circle.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:11:00.000
For more than 40 years, drivers and mechanics have stormed the beach at Daytona in search

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.310
of victory. But today, the newest cars are tested at another 40-year-old race

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:18.180
course. Cars as new and advanced as jet aircraft. Cars which explore new

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:26.970
boundaries of speed run at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway. Each year,

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:29.960
the best mechanics and drivers in the world

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:33.550
challenge the giant two and a half mile track

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:36.000
in the fastest race in the world,

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:47.300
the Indianapolis 500.

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:50.600
Here, the newest designs are tested.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:56.330
Here, in Gasoline Alley, the big cars are assembled.

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:00.000
Newest car is George Sally's number nine.

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:05.980
Its engine, placed on its side, for a lower center of gravity.

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:10.540
Let's hear what the driver Sam Hanks has to say about it.

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:15.350
George Solly has been thinking about this new design of his for some five years.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:19.160
George knows as much about an engine's performance as anybody in the business.

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:23.170
He works as a foreman at the Meyer and Drake factory, where they make most of the engines

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:24.800
that run at the Speedway.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:30.000
In last year's car, the one I bought into second place, and in this terrific new car, George

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.800
perfect circle piston rings. All the mechanics that have set up the winners here for as long

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:39.660
as I can remember have used perfect circle rings. It's the best way to get top performance

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:48.810
out of any engine as far as I'm concerned.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:54.650
The Purdue University band marches into the main straightaway to entertain 200,000 racing

00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:58.610
fans from 48 states and a dozen nations.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:06.380
this is the world's biggest paid admission sporting event reserved grandstand seats were

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:13.530
sold out months ago last night thousands slept in their cars outside the speedway then raced for

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:19.400
the general admission to the infield when the gates opened at five o'clock this morning the balloons

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:30.000
are released and the crowd suddenly grows still this is the day the hour the final minute tony holman

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:34.250
Speedway president speaks, the historic command.

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:42.130
- Gentlemen, start your engines!

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:53.150
- Mission winner, ready to lead them out.

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:56.740
The Mercury pace car swings onto the track.

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:58.800
The pace laugh begins.

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:05.160
In a new type start, all cars leave their pits single file and form into rows on the

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:07.460
track.

00:14:07.460 --> 00:14:11.980
From the tower terrace, overlooking the main straightaway, instructions are transmitted

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:18.770
by RCA equipment to camera crews stationed around the track.

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:23.390
End of the first turn, the Mercury pace car picks up speed in the short straightaway leading

00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:24.540
to the second turn.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:30.000
Driving the pace car is FC Reed, general manager of the Mercury division of

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:43.050
the ford motor company his only passenger is speedway president tony holman car tradition

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:48.810
started here 33 years ago when henry ford served as starter for the 1924 race

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:54.380
the parade swings up the long back stretch the next time these cars come through here

00:14:54.380 --> 00:15:00.000
they'll be traveling better than 175 miles an hour of the 33 cars that

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:03.680
in the starting lineup, 18, are new machines

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:06.240
in competition for the first time.

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:08.900
Through the third turn, the tension growing,

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:13.100
five drivers are in their first Indianapolis race.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:15.870
Here they come, the fourth turn,

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:18.590
and the field enters the main straightaway.

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:19.730
They'll hit the starting line

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:22.400
at better than 120 miles an hour.

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:39.200
The Mercury pace car streaks into the pit area and the race is on.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:45.810
O'Connor's number 12 roars away from the pole position to the lead.

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:48.720
The field settles into the groove in the first turn.

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:54.330
The second turn, O'Connor full throttle.

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:59.250
Up the back stretch, O'Connor moving away.

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:00.000
The third turn,

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:04.760
threat agabation challenging and as they move into the fourth turn, Troy Ruttman blasts

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:11.980
his way into third place. Jimmy Bryan's pit crew count off the leaders at the end of lap

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:25.910
one. The national champion is seventh. O'Connor is turning corners at 136 miles an hour. Heading

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:30.000
for the backstretch, agabation eases off as Ruttman sends A.J. Watson.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:35.520
in the machine screaming into second place.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:42.530
Rutman is coming fast.

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:44.510
Suddenly, the crowd roars.

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:47.670
Paul Russo has sent the Novi screaming through the field.

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:52.610
As Rutman takes the lead, the Novi special comes up to fourth.

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:55.600
Sam Hanks is fifth.

00:16:55.600 --> 00:17:00.000
Rutman tries to increase his lead, but on the backstretch, the V8 supercharged Novi

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:08.320
edges closer. Through the turns, the big Novi must hold position, but on the straightaway,

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:24.580
it travels 180 miles an hour and takes second place. Can the Novi get past Ruttman? Hank's

00:17:24.580 --> 00:17:28.520
little yellow car is fourth behind O'Connor and fights him down the backstretch.

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:34.770
Russo goes screaming into the main straightaway, alone.

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:36.800
Where's Ruttman?

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:43.820
Ruttman heading into the pits.

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:47.850
He's slipping out of his seat harness.

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:53.490
After only 30 miles, this new car is out of the race.

00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:56.430
Hanks goes screaming past O'Connor.

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:59.480
With Ruttman out, this puts Hanks in second place.

00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:09.850
The Novi mechanic wonders, is that little yellow car a challenge to the supercharged giant?

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:13.350
There, Hanks right behind him and gaining.

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:19.150
In a burst of straightaway speed, the Novi gains, but in the turns,

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:23.990
Hanks, in a lower, lighter car, closes in once more.

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:30.000
Coming onto the backstretch, Hanks leaves, but the Novi screams past him at 180 miles

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:31.890
The third turn.

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:34.420
Hanks passing to take the lead.

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:37.140
Now, can he hold it?

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:42.090
Hanks stays in first place.

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:44.470
Russo and the Novi can't catch him.

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:47.480
400 miles still to go.

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:52.050
Cutting through the field from 32nd starting position,

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:54.260
Jim Rathman in number 26.

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:58.420
His move, almost lost in the excitement of the duel for the lead.

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:09.660
Russo is in the pits for fuel and fresh tires.

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:13.670
Hanks is moving away.

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:16.190
Russo, 43 year old grandfather,

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:21.530
has 11 races behind him, but none tougher.

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:23.510
Refueled and three new tires,

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:26.680
the Novi rolls again in 43 seconds.

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:28.220
He's in second place.

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:30.000
Three quarters of a lap behind the fleet

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:42.170
Hanks, Rathman Pitts. The Chiropractic Special is a new car, the lightest on the track and

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:53.620
one of the fastest through the corners. Fuel, four tires, and away in 33 seconds. As Rathman

00:19:53.620 --> 00:20:00.000
hurls out of the pits, Russo screams by. Rathman's quick pit stop has brought him within

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:07.650
seconds of second place hanks cuts his way through the slower car stretching his lead before making

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:20.360
his first pit stop russo charging right behind him rathman closing in hanks heading for the pits

00:20:20.360 --> 00:20:29.730
he's covered the first 100 miles in 43 minutes a new record his average speed better than 141 miles an hour

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:31.480
But Hanks doesn't know it.

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:33.700
His crew has not been flashing his speed.

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:35.930
His instrument panel is disconnected.

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:39.450
It's up to Hanks and the experience of more than 4,000

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:44.390
miles in competition on this track.

00:20:44.390 --> 00:20:50.740
43 seconds, Hanks is away and in the lead.

00:20:50.740 --> 00:20:53.080
Designer, mechanic, owner.

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:56.440
George Sally's every hope is running in that machine.

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:04.360
Russo's Novi still in second, but right behind him,

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:10.530
Rathman in number 26 is closing in.

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:14.350
Rathman passing.

00:21:14.350 --> 00:21:15.930
He's in second place.

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:21.920
And his crew sense he's just beginning to run.

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:25.940
Rathman's chiropractic special roars away after Hanks.

00:21:25.940 --> 00:21:29.410
Hanks' crew warned the front runner Rathman is gaining.

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:38.970
spins. Al Keller in the Bardall special spins into the wall. Johnny Thompson misses a head-on

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:44.260
crash by inches after turning a complete loop in the infield. Keller's not injured, but the field

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:51.300
is slowed until the track is cleared. Rathman pits for the second time. His pit crew sets to

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:58.290
work changing three tires and refueling. These men have made a science out of pit stops.

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:22.370
10 seconds, 20 seconds, 30 seconds, Rathman is on his way in 33 seconds.

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:52.450
is in for his second pit stop, with Rathman less than a half a lap behind. Rathman takes the lead.

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:59.960
Hanks is on his way in second place, less than 10 seconds behind and a job to do.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.980
to catch the fastest man in the race.

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:10.250
Rathman has turned one lap at 143.4 miles an hour.

00:23:10.250 --> 00:23:12.210
Hanks is getting traction like a mail train

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:18.040
coming through the corners.

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:28.770
Hanks closes in at the 350 mile mark.

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:30.000
Coming through the second turn,

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:35.830
up the pass, Hanks takes back the lead. That new design works.

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:06.640
Uh-oh, Don Edmonds spins out.

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:11.130
Only 18 of the starting field of 33 are still running.

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:14.690
The yellow flag slows the race until the track is cleared.

00:24:14.690 --> 00:24:21.760
Hanks has a 10-second lead and only 15 laps to go.

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:30.000
The track is cleared

00:24:30.090 --> 00:24:34.360
speed but Rathman's crew tell him to take it easy they want to be sure he'll

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:40.940
finish Hanks is running like a green turning the last few laps at 141 miles

00:24:40.940 --> 00:24:46.470
an hour an incredible performance Alice Hanks waits behind the pit railing just

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:53.620
a few feet from George Sally and the crew the white flag is out Hanks begins

00:24:53.620 --> 00:25:00.000
his last lap Jim Rathman's car

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.580
special firmly in control of second place Rathman's run from 30 seconds

00:25:04.580 --> 00:25:09.770
starting position has been a beauty hanks coming off the corners like a

00:25:09.770 --> 00:25:17.450
gunshot is roaring for the checkered flag Sam Hanks wins the Indianapolis 500

00:25:17.450 --> 00:25:27.700
21 seconds later Rathman takes the checker and one lap behind Jimmy Bryan

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:29.670
national champion places third

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:33.000
One minute ahead of Paul Russo's Novi running fourth.

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:39.040
Here he comes, the 42-year-old winner of the 41st Indianapolis 500.

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:43.260
And there isn't a man along the pit wall that isn't happy for him.

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:49.080
His average speed for 500 miles was better than 135 miles an hour.

00:25:49.460 --> 00:25:52.180
Five miles an hour faster than the old record.

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:56.240
There are tears in his eyes as Hank squeals into Victory Lane

00:25:56.240 --> 00:26:00.000
where his wife, his pit crew, and George Sally wait for him.

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.520
This is a dream come true for

00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:04.960
driver and mechanic. Other men

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:06.870
designing still newer

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:08.030
experimental machines

00:26:08.030 --> 00:26:10.020
will take heart from this victory.

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:12.420
For men who build and

00:26:12.420 --> 00:26:14.360
design automobiles are never

00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:16.420
content. Hank's share

00:26:16.420 --> 00:26:18.360
of the $300,000 purse,

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:19.860
the largest in racing,

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:22.560
is more than $103,000

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:24.220
which, added

00:26:24.220 --> 00:26:26.440
to prize money won in past 500s,

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:28.160
makes Hank's the biggest

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:30.000
money winner in Indianapolis

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:35.200
With this victory, Hanks announced his retirement from Speedway competition.

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:40.540
His experimental car proved it could go the distance against top challengers.

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:49.050
Sam Hanks, his crew, his car, have earned their place in Victory Circle.

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:59.670
Sam Hanks' car and eight of the first ten finishers were equipped with perfect circle piston rings, the brand most people prefer.
